# Build and validate the Agentic SDLC three-tier task board

<!-- markdownlint-enable MD025 -->

## Ask

Create a disposable, local-only three-tier web application named SDLC Task
Board and ship it through the complete Agentic SDLC workflow. Do not push,
publish, create a pull request, or depend on a non-local runtime service.

## Outcome

Deliver a browser task board backed by a versioned Django/Gunicorn JSON API and
PostgreSQL. The promoted clean Git revision must run as exactly two healthy
Docker Compose services and pass semantic, automated, and headless Playwright Test UAT.

## Context

The logical tiers are a semantic HTML/CSS/JavaScript browser GUI, a Python
Django application served by Gunicorn, and PostgreSQL with a committed
migration. The runtime presentation assets may be served by the web container;
logical tiers do not imply three containers.

The Compose project is `{{COMPOSE_PROJECT}}`. Let Compose apply its canonical
`com.docker.compose.project` and `com.docker.compose.service` labels. Apply the
custom label `sdlc-workflow-test.verification-id={{VERIFICATION_ID}}` to the web
image, both services, the private network, and the database volume. Do not
manually declare labels in Docker's reserved `com.docker.compose` namespace.

## Constraints

- Run the web application and PostgreSQL as exactly two Compose services named
  `web` and `db`.
- Create and validate the Compose files, but do not invoke Docker Compose from
  an SDLC phase. The outer verifier owns every live Compose action through its
  verification-generation lock.
- Bind a Docker-assigned web port to loopback only; never publish PostgreSQL.
- Generate database credentials in a private mode-0600 environment file under
  `{{PRIVATE_ROOT}}`; inject it through environment and never commit or print it.
- Keep prompts, plans, run state, transcripts, and raw logs under
  `{{PRIVATE_ROOT}}`, using its isolated Codex home.
- Keep scenario evidence and screenshots under `{{EVIDENCE_ROOT}}`.
- Limit writes to the disposable project `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}`, the private and
  evidence roots, the named Compose project, its exactly labelled resources,
  loopback HTTP, and fresh verifier-owned headless Chrome processes and isolated contexts.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Users can create and list tasks; blank titles are rejected in the GUI and
  API without a database write.
- [ ] Users can mark a task complete and filter active or completed tasks.
- [ ] GUI and API expose stable record IDs and completion state for independent
  API and PostgreSQL correlation.
- [ ] Health checks, database readiness, non-root web execution, deterministic
  dependency pins, and a committed migration are present.
- [ ] Unit, API, PostgreSQL, migration, vertical HTTP, and headless Playwright Test GUI UAT
  pass against the clean promoted revision.
- [ ] README and CHANGELOG document local start, endpoint discovery, health,
  browser, reset, and troubleshooting.

## Verification

Use test-first development. Product-owned tests remain separate from the
verifier-owned acceptance bundle. The frozen interface for independent checks is:

- Heading `Task board`; textbox labelled `Task title`; buttons `Add task`,
  `Active`, and `Completed`; visible `role="alert"` for blank-title validation.
- Each row has `data-task-id` equal to its positive integer database ID and a
  checkbox labelled `Complete task` exposing the saved completion state.
- `GET /api/v1/tasks` returns an array of objects containing `id` (integer),
  `title` (string), and `completed` (boolean).
- PostgreSQL table `tasks_task` has `id`, `title`, and `completed` columns.

Use headless Playwright Test with Chrome for required web acceptance. Run
four fresh owned stages in order: `capability-discovery`, `evaluate`,
`uat-before-restart`, and `uat-after-restart`. Each stage starts a new process
and isolated context, records assertions, screenshots, a trace, actual browser
version and timestamps, and closes its processes even with `--keep`. Native
desktop capture, an unlocked screen, display selection, and foreground windows
are not prerequisites. Optional headless isolated Playwright MCP exploration
is separate from the acceptance oracle and cannot satisfy a required stage.

The verifier freezes its independent test bundle before product implementation;
never copy it into product TDD tests or alter it to fit an implementation.
Run evaluation against the clean registered integration revision and UAT against
the clean promoted revision. The outer lifecycle owner alone restarts Compose
between the two UAT stages, retaining the original database volume. Rediscover
the dynamic loopback port, refresh recorded endpoints, then launch a fresh
post-restart browser against the same task ID/title/completion state.
Independent API and PostgreSQL checks accompany blank rejection, creation,
completion, and post-restart persistence. Use the frozen task-board DOM/API/table
contract in the generated prompt. Before evaluation and separately before UAT,
use the declared disposable reset to establish an empty database; resets are
fixture setup, never part of the product acceptance journey. Never reset between
UAT stages.

A failed stage remains failed and its artifacts are preserved. Correct the
proven owner and start a new trial; no later success erases the earlier result.
Resume retained application work with a fresh browser, never a retained tab.
Laptops must remain awake with Docker and the agent running. A locked-screen
certification additionally needs independently observed lock intervals and an
actual agent launching a new stage and collecting evidence before unlock;
a standalone browser smoke test does not certify agent continuity.

Capture five independently produced sanitized screenshots under `{{EVIDENCE_ROOT}}`: empty
state, validation error, created task, completed filter, and post-restart. Write
the `agentic-sdlc/three-tier-results-v3` semantic manifest incrementally to
`{{EVIDENCE_ROOT}}/three-tier-results.json`.

## Live Experiment Environment

The environment is disposable, non-production, and identified by verification
ID `{{VERIFICATION_ID}}`. Cleanup may remove only the exact owned project,
private/evidence roots, owned browser processes, and Docker resources whose IDs
and ownership labels were recorded by the lifecycle helper.

## Non-goals

- Git reads may use only the verifier-owned local bare origin through normal
  SDLC admission. No pushes, external Git operations, pull requests, publication,
  cloud services, or non-local runtime dependencies. Build-time downloads are
  limited to the fixed public base images and pinned public Python packages
  from PyPI and the pinned verifier Playwright packages from npm, including their
  transitive dependencies. All application behavior
  must run locally after the build.
- No host-published PostgreSQL port or additional runtime service.
- No committed credentials, prompts, raw logs, private state, or screenshots.

